dark-energy.imported-macdowell-mansouri-.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), and adjoin to the vectors of $\Cl(1,3)$ one vector $V$ orthogonal to them, of fixed squared norm $Q(V) = -\ell^2$, with $\ell$ the de Sitter radius, $\ell^2 = 3/\Lambda$ (taken with its sign: on the anti-de Sitter branch below $\ell^{2} < 0$, so $Q(V) = -\ell^{2} > 0$ and every display holds unchanged): the vectors of $\Cl(1,4)$ are the fibre of the de Sitter group $SO(1,4)$, of signature $(1,4)$, and the stabiliser of $V$ is the Lorentz group of $V^\perp$, the vectors of $\Cl(1,3)$. Let $A$ be a connection for $SO(1,4)$, a gauge field $A(a)$ with values in the bivectors of $\Cl(1,4)$ (over flat spacetime the rank-five bundle is trivial), and $F$ its field strength. In the gauge where $V$ is constant the connection splits as
\[
A(a) \;=\; \Omega(a) - \hb^{-1}(a)\wedge V/\ell^{2}
\]
(normalisation as stored), with $\Omega$ the Lorentz connection on $V^\perp$ and the soldering form the physical frame $\hb^{-1}$, which is the covariant derivative of $V$, $D_a V = \hb^{-1}(a)$; the field strength splits into a Lorentz block, which on physical bivectors is $\mathcal{F}(B) = -(\mathcal{R}(B) - \ell^{-2}B)$ in the store's sign of the curvature operator and vanishes exactly on de Sitter space, and a translational block (the bivectors containing $V$) proportional to the torsion. In the forms of the sources, $A = \omega + e$, the Lorentz block is $F^{ab} = R^{ab} + \ell^{-2}\,e^a\wedge e^b$, which is exactly the operator $\mathcal{F}(B) = -(\mathcal{R}(B) - \ell^{-2}B)$ (the Riemann endomorphism being $R(u,v) = -\mathcal{R}(u \wedge v)$ as a bivector, and $(e^a \wedge e^b)(u,v)$ acting as $(u \wedge v) \cdot w$), and the torsion is $T^a = D e^a$. The functional
\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{\mathrm{MM}}[A, V] \;&\sim\; \ell^{2}\int \epsilon_{abcd}\,F^{ab}\wedge F^{cd}\\
&=\; \ell^{2}\!\int \epsilon_{abcd}R^{ab}\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; 2\!\int \epsilon_{abcd}\, e^a\wedge e^b\wedge R^{cd} \;+\; \ell^{-2}\!\int \epsilon_{abcd}\, e^a\wedge e^b\wedge e^c\wedge e^d ,
\end{aligned}
\]
where $\epsilon_{abcd}$ is the fibre volume form of the rank-five bundle contracted with the unit vector $V/\ell$, decomposes as displayed into the Gauss-Bonnet term, the Einstein-Cartan term and the cosmological term; in geometric algebra it is $\ell^{2}\int \det(\hb)^{-1}\,(-2)\,\langle\mathcal{F}(a \wedge b)\,I\,\mathcal{F}(I\partial_b \wedge \partial_a)\rangle\,d^{4}x$ up to the stored normalisation, over the background region. Its Euler-Lagrange equations, from varying $\omega$ and $e$, are the Einstein-Cartan equations
\begin{equation}
\epsilon_{abcd}\, e^{c}\wedge T^{d} \;=\; 0, \qquad \epsilon_{abcd}\, e^{b}\wedge\bigl(R^{cd} + \ell^{-2} e^{c}\wedge e^{d}\bigr) \;=\; 0 ,
\end{equation}
which on the invertible region are equivalent to vanishing torsion, $D \wedge \hb^{T}(a) = \hb^{T}(\nabla \wedge a)$, together with the Einstein equations with cosmological constant $\Lambda$, $\mathcal{G}(a) + \Lambda a = 0$. The anti-de Sitter branch, $\Lambda < 0$, reads identically with the group $SO(2,3)$ on a fibre of signature $(2,3)$ (the vectors of $\Cl(1,3)$ with an adjoined $V$ of positive square), $Q(V) = -3/\Lambda > 0$, and the Lorentz group as the stabiliser of $V$; the identity $Q(I)\,Q(V) = -1$ is what the reduction uses, and it is sign-independent. \emph{Provenance:} \cite{MacDowellMansouri1977, StelleWest1980}; the Cartan-geometric form and the contraction $\ell \to \infty$ are \cite{Wise2010}; the variational identity is used at assembly level, and is declared as such here. The sign of the $\ell^{-2}e\wedge e$ term in these conventions is fixed by Lemma \claimref{dark-energy.stelle-west-vector-frozen-point}(iv). The reading on flat spacetime, with the soldering form as the physical frame, is the store's transcription of the cited theorem into Gauge Theory Gravity.

dark-energy.under-action-written-norm-pa.formal:
In the three-argument functional of Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation}, $\Lambda$ enters as the value of the Stelle-West constraints $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$, $Q(V) = -3/\Lambda$, a number fixed before variation: it is the index $\mu$ of Definition \claimref{obj.dark-energy.four-ways-number-enters-theory-shape}. Consequently:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] with $\Lambda$ fixed, the constrained fluctuation space of $(I, V)$ has dimension nine and the nine broken generators act onto it bijectively (Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.point-infinity-has-no-physical-fluctuation}): no fluctuation of the norm exists, every solution with parameter $\Lambda$ has $H^2 = Q(I) = \Lambda/3$ on its de Sitter region, and $N = M_P\sqrt{3/\Lambda}$ is constant on each $\mathcal{S}_\Lambda$;
\item[(ii)] $N$ varies with $\Lambda$ across the family, $d(N^2)/d\Lambda = -3M_P^2/\Lambda^2 \neq 0$, so it is not (D); and it is not (K), by the rescaling argument of Fact \claimref{standard-model.trichotomy-what-kind-number};
\item[(iii)] therefore $N$ is (P), a parameter of the laws, and not (I): clause (3) of the Standard-Model paper's classification, ``$N$ is initial data'', is false for the action as written.
\end{enumerate}
If instead $\Lambda$ is allowed to vary, the linearised constraints become three independent conditions on the thirteen components $(\delta I, \delta V, \delta\Lambda)$, the constrained space has dimension ten, the gauge orbit is still nine-dimensional (bivectors do not change a norm), and exactly one non-gauge direction appears: the norm mode
\[
(\delta I, \delta V, \delta\Lambda) \;\propto\; \Bigl(\frac{I}{2\Lambda},\ -\frac{V}{2\Lambda},\ 1\Bigr),
\]
which is the tangent at $s = 1$ of the rescaling family $(sI, V/s, s^2\Lambda)$ and which needs an equation of motion the action does not supply.

obj.dark-energy.four-ways-number-enters-.formal:
Let a theory consist of a kinematic structure $\mathcal{K}$ with a notion of isomorphism, a family of action functionals $S_\mu$ indexed by parameters $\mu$ on a configuration space carrying $\mathcal{K}$, with solution spaces $\mathcal{S}_\mu$ and an evolution on each. A real quantity $q$ defined on $\bigcup_\mu \mathcal{S}_\mu$ and conserved by the evolution is called
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(K)] \emph{kinematic} if it factors through the kinematic isomorphism class: $q(x) = q(y)$ whenever $x, y$ have isomorphic kinematic structures;
\item[(D)] \emph{dynamically fixed} if it is constant on $\bigcup_\mu \mathcal{S}_\mu$;
\item[(P)] \emph{a parameter of the laws} if it is constant on each $\mathcal{S}_\mu$ but not on the union: $q$ is a function of $\mu$ and of nothing else;
\item[(I)] \emph{initial data} if it is not constant on some $\mathcal{S}_\mu$.
\end{enumerate}

odd-extensions.are-ascents-pair-signed.formal:
In the tractor bundle of signature $(2,4)$ let $I$ be the frozen point, $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$, and for $\Lambda \neq 0$ let $V$ be the Stelle-West vector. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $I$ and $V$ span a hyperbolic plane: $\ip{I}{V} = 0$, $Q(I)$ and $Q(V)$ have opposite signs, and the unit directions $e_{I}$, $e_{V}$ anticommute with each other and with the Lorentz frame of $\{I,V\}^{\perp}$;
\item[(ii)] $I^{\perp}$ is spanned by the Lorentz frame and $e_{V}$, and $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ is the subalgebra of the rung generated by these five: the space extension $\Cl(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$ ($e_{V}^{2} = -1$) and the time extension $\Cl(2,3)$ for $\Lambda < 0$ ($e_{V}^{2} = +1$); adjoining $e_{I}$ returns the whole rung, so the ascent factors as
\[
\Cl(1,3) \;\subset\; \Cl(I^{\perp}) \;\subset\; \Cl(2,4),
\]
with the sign of $\Lambda$ deciding which of the pair's two signed directions is adjoined first;
\item[(iii)] at $\Lambda = 0$ the frozen point, written $I_{0}$ from here on, is null and lies in its own complement, $I^{\perp}$ is degenerate of signature $(1,3;1)$, and $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(1,3;1)$ is the degenerate algebra with $I$ itself the nilpotent generator: $I^{2} = 0$, $I$ anticommutes with the Lorentz frame, and $I\,\Cl(1,3;1)$ is a sixteen-dimensional ideal of square zero;
\item[(iv)] the Lorentz frame $\{I,V\}^{\perp}$ has signature $(1,3)$ for every value of $\Lambda$, including zero.
\end{enumerate}

odd-extensions.translation-bivectors-fro.formal:
Under Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-frozen-point}, write $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ for the Clifford bundle of the rank-five bundle $I^{\perp}$, and let $e_{5} = V/|V|$ be the unit Stelle-West direction, so that the Lorentz frame $e_{1}, \ldots, e_{4}$ of $\{I,V\}^{\perp}$ and $e_{5}$ together generate $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ fibrewise. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] for $\Lambda > 0$, $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(1,4)$, $e_{5}^{2} = -1$, and the translation bivectors $e_{\mu}e_{5}$ of the MacDowell-Mansouri connection generate this world's shadow $\Cl(1,3)$;
\item[(ii)] for $\Lambda < 0$, $\Cl(I^{\perp}) \cong \Cl(2,3)$, $e_{5}^{2} = +1$, and the translation bivectors generate a copy of the mirror's abstract algebra $\Cl(3,1)$, while this world's shadow is the subalgebra generated by the Lorentz frame (what this copy is and is not: Proposition \claimref{odd-extensions.belongs-tower} and Corollary \claimref{odd-extensions.two-readings-which-which}).
\end{enumerate}
In either case the fibrewise algebra $\Cl(I^{\perp})$ is one of the two extensions of Corollary \claimref{odd-extensions.four-extensions-oriented-pair}, and the Poincar\'e contraction $\ell \to \infty$ leaves the Clifford type of the translation algebra unchanged.

odd-extensions.transport-constrains-froz.formal:
Let $W = I_{0}^{\perp}$, the degenerate space of signature $(1,3;1)$ at $\Lambda = 0$ with radical spanned by the null frozen point $I_{0}$, written $I$ in this theorem, and let $\widetilde W$ be its minimal completion. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\widetilde W$ has signature $(2,4)$ and is the rung; the isometry of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-minimal-completion-degenerate-algebra-rule} fixing $W$ pointwise is unique, so the null partner $\varphi$ of $I$ with $Q(I,\varphi) = 1$, $Q(\varphi,\varphi) = 0$ and $\varphi$ orthogonal to the Lorentz frame of $W$ is canonical: the transport supplies the hyperbolic plane $\mathrm{span}(I, \varphi)$ together with a distinguished null basis of it;
\item[(ii)] every vector orthogonal to the Lorentz frame is $aI + b\varphi$ with norm $2ab$; a frozen point of cosmological constant $\Lambda$ is such a vector with $2ab = \Lambda/3$, and the sign of $\Lambda$ is the sign of the product of its components along the null generator and its canonical partner;
\item[(iii)] the isometries of the completion that fix the Lorentz frame act on the plane as $O(1,1)$, whose orbits on nonzero vectors are exactly the level sets of the norm; so the only invariant of the frozen point under the symmetries the transport supplies is its norm $\Lambda/3$: the cosmological constant, sign and magnitude, is the one modulus of the frozen point that the transport leaves free;
\item[(iv)] no symmetry of the completion carries a frozen point of positive norm to one of negative norm, and the two intermediate algebras $\Cl(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp})$ for the two signs are non-isomorphic (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.twistor-algebra}(iv)); the two signs are inequivalent under every symmetry the construction has, and the choice between them is decided by nothing in the transport.
\end{enumerate}

